        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.22 Web-based Internet Information and Applications</td>
        <td>Refer to attached VPAT</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.23 Telecommunications Products</td>
        <td>Not Applicable</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.24 Video and Multi-media Products</td>
        <td>Refer to attached VPAT</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.25 Self-Contained, Closed Products</td>
        <td>Not Applicable</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.26 Desktop and Portable Computers</td>
        <td>Refer to attached VPAT</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.31 Functional Performance Criteria</td>
        <td>Refer to attached VPAT</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Section 1194.41 Information, Documentation and Support</td>
        <td>Refer to attached VPAT</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 20px;">Section 1194.21 Software Applications and Operating Systems – Detail</h2>
  <h3 style="font-size: 18px;">VPAT™</h3>
  <h3 style="font-size: 18px;">Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Criteria</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Supporting Features</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Remarks & Explanations</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>(a) When software is designed to run on a system that has a keyboard, product functions shall be executable from a keyboard where the function itself or the result of performing a function can be discerned textually.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>When the Mediasite Player is first launched, a screen reader, if running on the computer, prompts the user to type a keyboard shortcut to enter screen reader mode. Once a user opts into the screen reader mode, the preference is retained for subsequent presentation playback via a cookie option.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(b) Applications shall not disrupt or disable activated features of other products that are identified as accessibility features, where those features are developed and documented according to industry standards. Applications also shall not disrupt or disable activated features of any operating system that are identified as accessibility features where the application programming interface for those accessibility features has been documented by the manufacturer of the operating system and is available to the product developer.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>The Mediasite Player requires active focus to use keyboard shortcuts. We do not expect Mediasite Player's keyboard shortcuts to interfere with any accessibility features presented by any other product or operating system.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(c) A well-defined on-screen indication of the current focus shall be provided that moves among interactive interface</td>
        <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player provides some on-screen indication of</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Criteria</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Supporting Features</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Remarks & Explanations</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>(d) Elements as the input focus changes. The focus shall be programmatically exposed so that Assistive Technology can track focus and focus changes.</td>
        <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
        <td>The current focus and tracking of same throughout the software. However, Mediasite does not support Microsoft Active Accessibility (MSAA).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(e) Sufficient information about a user interface element including the identity, operation and state of the element shall be available to Assistive Technology. When an image represents a program element, the information conveyed by the image must also be available in text.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player displays slides as images that are associated with the video. The player supports a screen reader mode. When the player is in screen reader mode, the player displays slide titles and slide descriptions of the active slide. It is expected that slide titles and slide descriptions are authored for the content being played back in the Mediasite Player.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(f) When bitmap images are used to identify controls, status indicators, or other programmatic elements, the meaning assigned to those images shall be consistent throughout an application's performance.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player uses standard and consistent images throughout its applications.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(g) Textual information shall be provided through operating system functions for displaying text. The minimum information that shall be made available is text content, text input caret location, and text attributes.</td>
        <td>Supported with Minor Exceptions</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player uses standard system functions to send textual information to the operating system in most cases.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(h) Applications shall not override user selected contrast and color selections and other individual display attributes.</td>
        <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player maintains user selected contrast and color selections in many cases.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(i) When animation is displayed, the information shall be displayable in at least one non-animated</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player supports a screen reader mode that does</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(j) Color coding shall not be used as the only means of conveying information, prompting a response, or distinguishing a visual element.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player supports an additional theme based on a light background. However, Mediasite Player does not support themes in screen reader mode.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(k) When a product permits a user to adjust color and contrast settings, a variety of color selections capable of producing a range of contrast levels shall be provided.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td>Mediasite Player does not support adjusting color and contrast settings for the application. Users producing content need to account for using appropriate colors and range of contrast levels.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(l) Software shall not use flashing or blinking text, objects, or other elements having a frequency greater than 2 Hz and lower than 55 Hz.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(m) When electronic forms are used, the form shall allow people using Assistive Technology to access the information, field elements, and functionality required for completion and submission of the form, including all directions and cues.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <h2 style="font-size: 20px;">Section 1194.22 Web-based Internet Information and Applications – Detail</h2>
  <h3 style="font-size: 18px;">VPAT™</h3>
  <h3 style="font-size: 18px;">Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Criteria</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Supporting Features</th>
        <th style="width: 50%;">Remarks & Explanations</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>(a) A text equivalent for every non-text element shall be provided (e.g., via "alt", "longdesc", or in element content).</td>
        <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
        <td>Some graphic elements like slides require text descriptions to be authored. Available only through post-production.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(b) Equivalent alternatives for any multimedia presentation shall be synchronized with the presentation.</td>
        <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
        <td>Closed captioning support for live broadcasts is available through third party solutions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(c) Web pages shall be designed so that all information conveyed with color is also available without color, for example from context or markup.</td>
        <td>Supported</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>(d) Documents shall be organized so they are readable</td>
        <td>Not Supported</td>
        <td></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="margin-top: 30px;">Note to 1194.22: The Board interprets paragraphs (a) through (k) of this section as consistent with the following priority 1 Checkpoints of the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines 1.0 (WCAG 1.0) (May 5 1999) published by the Web Accessibility Initiative of the World Wide Web Consortium: Paragraph (a) - 1.1, (b) - 1.4, (c) - 2.1, (d) - 6.1, (e) - 1.2, (f) - 9.1, (g) - 5.1, (h) - 5.2, (i) - 12.1, (j) - 7.1, (k) - 11.4.</p>
</div>

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Accessibility Template</title>
<style>
  @page { size: A4; margin: 0; }
  .page {
    width: 794px; height: 1123px;
    padding: 50px 60px; box-sizing: border-box;
    overflow: hidden; page-break-after: always;
    position: relative;
  }
  .page:last-child { page-break-after: auto; }
  body {
    font-family: "DejaVu Serif", "Liberation Serif", Georgia, "Helvetica", Arial, sans-serif;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  h1, h2, h3 {
    font-weight: bold;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin-top: 15px;
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }
  td, th {
    padding: 10px 0;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    text-align: left;
  }
  .header-row th {
    background-color: #f0f0f0;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="page">
  <div>
    <h2>Section 1194.24 Video and Multi-media Products – Detail</h2>
    <h3>VPAT™</h3>
    <p><strong>Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</strong></p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr class="header-row">
          <th>Criteria</th>
          <th>Supporting Features</th>
          <th>Remarks & Explanations</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_a">{{ criteria_a }}</span></td>
          <td>Not Applicable</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_b">{{ criteria_b }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_c">{{ criteria_c }}</span></td>
          <td><span data-field="supporting_c">{{ supporting_c }}</span></td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_d">{{ criteria_d }}</span></td>
          <td><span data-field="supporting_d">{{ supporting_d }}</span></td>
          <td><span data-field="remarks_d">{{ remarks_d }}</span></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_e">{{ criteria_e }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td><span data-field="remarks_e">{{ remarks_e }}</span></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div>
    <h2>Section 1194.26 Desktop and Portable Computers – Detail</h2>
    <h3>VPAT™</h3>
    <p><strong>Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</strong></p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr class="header-row">
          <th>Criteria</th>
          <th>Supporting Features</th>
          <th>Remarks & Explanations</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_a">{{ criteria_a }}</span></td>
          <td>Not Supported</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_b">{{ criteria_b }}</span></td>
          <td>Not Supported</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_c">{{ criteria_c }}</span></td>
          <td>Not Supported</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_d">{{ criteria_d }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div>
    <h2>Section 1194.31 Functional Performance Criteria – Detail</h2>
    <h3>VPAT™</h3>
    <p><strong>Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</strong></p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr class="header-row">
          <th>Criteria</th>
          <th>Supporting Features</th>
          <th>Remarks & Explanations</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_a">{{ criteria_a }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td>Supported via screen reader layout.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_b">{{ criteria_b }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td>Audio is available, captioning is available, screen magnifiers could be used to enlarge slides. Third-party applications like screen readers may also be used. Keyboard shortcuts are also available.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_c">{{ criteria_c }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported</td>
          <td>Browser zooming can also be used. In addition, zooming is supported for slides in the Mediasite Player when not in the screen reader layout.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_d">{{ criteria_d }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported with Minor Exceptions</td>
          <td>Supported through SAMI, DFXP or SRT files with post-production support. Live broadcasts need support from third party captioning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_e">{{ criteria_e }}</span></td>
          <td>Not Applicable</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_f">{{ criteria_f }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
          <td>Mediasite Player, when operating in the screen reader layout does not need fine motor control or simultaneous actions.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div>
    <h2>Section 1194.41 Information, Documentation and Support – Detail</h2>
    <h3>VPAT™</h3>
    <p><strong>Voluntary Product Accessibility Template®</strong></p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr class="header-row">
          <th>Criteria</th>
          <th>Supporting Features</th>
          <th>Remarks & Explanations</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_a">{{ criteria_a }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
          <td>End users (viewers) of content created by Mediasite are not supported. Beyond our documentation, end users of Mediasite Recorders and the Mediasite Enterprise Video Platform who have a support contract with Sonic Foundry can receive auditory help by calling our Tech Support phone number.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_b">{{ criteria_b }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
          <td>End users (viewers) of content created by Mediasite are not supported. End users of Mediasite Recorders and the Mediasite Enterprise Video Platform who have a support contract with Sonic Foundry can receive auditory help by calling our Tech Support phone number. A technical planner on accessibility is also available in text format.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span data-field="criteria_c">{{ criteria_c }}</span></td>
          <td>Supported with Exceptions</td>
          <td>Customers under a support contract have multiple means of communicating with Sonic Foundry support staff including email or phone. A searchable knowledge base of online articles is also available.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>